Subject: Delete The Element-Store Stack Template

What this does
  Every computed element store used to be compiled through a fixed eleven-
  instruction stack-shuffling template, so `function f(a,i,v){ a[i]=v; }` became
  sixteen opcodes. It is deleted; `[obj][key][val] put_array_el` is now the whole
  store, five opcodes in that example.

Why we need it
  The template existed to run ToPropertyKey before the right-hand side whenever
  the base was not nullish. That is the pre-ES2021 order, from when ToPropertyKey
  ran while the Reference Record was built; ES2024 6.2.5.6 PutValue does both
  coercions after both sides are evaluated, and OP_put_array_el already performs
  both (ToObject then ToPropertyKey). Deleting the template therefore removes
  work and moves the observable coercion order onto node's.

What it adds
  Nothing: no opcode, no slot spent, no format change. Six OP_swap (twelve operand
  stack loads and stores), two OP_to_propkey, a OP_dup, an
  OP_is_undefined_or_null and a not-taken branch disappear per store site, and
  the redundant OP_to_propkey2 that get_lvalue() emitted for this lvalue is
  dropped too. Measured on a store-heavy row: 22,011,000 of 64,032,185
  dispatches removed, 107 ms to 73 ms.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No. The built-in blobs are deliberately not regenerated: their stores use
  integer keys on arrays the builtin itself created, where the old and new
  orders cannot differ.

